---
import { ALL_TRANSLATED } from '../../i18n/resolve'
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PageToolbar from '../../components/PageToolbar.astro'
import { withRoot } from '../../lib/site-root'
import { tmuxManualUrl } from '../../lib/tmux-manual-data'

const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'libtmux', url: withRoot('/') }, { name: 'Support and release stages', url: withRoot('/meta/support/') }]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Support and release stages"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  description="What the concept badge and the release stages on libtmux.org mean, and how to report a failing example."
  localeStatuses={ALL_TRANSLATED}
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <PageToolbar breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} />
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Support and release stages</h1>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Menus and install pages label each product with a badge. This page says what the badges mean.
    </p>

    <h2 id="concept-and-alpha" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Concept and alpha</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      <strong>Concept</strong> is a support scope. The MCP servers and workspace builders for every
      language except Python are maintained examples of using libtmux in a real application. They
      carry no support or compatibility commitment, and their APIs, MCP tools, command output and
      workspace configuration can change. Pin an example and the core library together when you adapt
      one.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      <strong>Alpha</strong> is a release stage. Publishing a package or adding commands does not
      change an example's concept scope, so an alpha core library can have concept examples beside it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="release-stages" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Release stages</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      A badge shows the stage that the package's registry declares, or the stage of the newest
      version the registry lists: <strong>pre-alpha</strong>, <strong>alpha</strong>,
      <strong>beta</strong>, <strong>rc</strong> or <strong>stable</strong>. When the registry lists no
      release at all, the badge shows the stage of the repository's newest release tag instead, and a
      tag that is exactly <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>, with an optional leading <code>v</code> or
      package label, such as <code>v0.1.0</code>, reads <strong>stable</strong>. A tag newer than the
      registry's newest version does not count: a registry that lists only <code>0.1.0-alpha.2</code>
      shows <strong>alpha</strong> even after the repository tags <code>v0.1.0</code>. A package with
      no release and no release tag shows <strong>unreleased</strong>. A language without an
      implementation has no badge.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Python's <code>tmuxp</code> workspace loader and Python MCP server show the stage their PyPI
      projects declare instead of <strong>concept</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="use-an-example" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Use an example</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Open the MCP or Workspace Manager entry for your language. Its install page names the package or
      source build, the runtime it needs and the tmux endpoint it talks to.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      An MCP tool can send shell input, and a workspace file can configure shell commands. Keep tmux
      access local or behind access controls you own. A concept badge adds no authentication,
      authorization or isolation. If you run an example in production, you own its configuration
      compatibility, upgrades, access controls and monitoring.
    </p>

    <h2 id="report-a-failure" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Report a failure</h2>
    <p class="mt-3">
      An example has to work at its documented revision. Load a workspace and inspect the session, or
      connect an MCP client and call a tool, against a
      <a class="underline" href={`${tmuxManualUrl('latest', 'full')}#DESCRIPTION`}>separate tmux server</a>
      so your own sessions stay untouched. A page that renders or a package that installs does not
      show that the example works.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      Report the revision, the tmux version, the smallest input that fails and the error you saw. If
      the failure comes from the core library, reduce it to a core API call: an example's concept
      scope does not excuse a broken core contract.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
